WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.911
Chapter five: Rome. When Wren is the emperor.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:10.390
Two thousand years ago, Rome paid its soldiers in silver coins called denarii.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:14.763
Under the first emperor, Augustus, they were almost pure silver.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:19.792
Rome ran on its army. A dying emperor reportedly told his sons:

00:00:19.792 --> 00:00:23.283
keep the soldiers rich, never mind anyone else.

00:00:23.632 --> 00:00:29.389
Soldiers cost more silver than the empire had. So emperors did what Wren did:

00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:33.052
melt the coins, add cheap copper, stamp out more.

00:00:33.402 --> 00:00:37.316
By the year two hundred, coins were about half silver.

00:00:37.316 --> 00:00:42.246
By two seventy, under five percent: copper, with a thin silver skin.

00:00:43.197 --> 00:00:49.117
In Roman Egypt, a sack of wheat cost about eight drachmas in the year one hundred.

00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.294
Prices crept up for a long time, then leapt:

00:00:52.294 --> 00:00:55.038
by three hundred, about three hundred.

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:00.537
Picture savings worth a year of bread, back then.

00:01:00.537 --> 00:01:06.198
By three hundred, the same sum bought about ten days. Nobody stole a coin.

00:01:06.198 --> 00:01:08.264
The emperor just made more.

00:01:09.213 --> 00:01:16.570
In three hundred and one, Emperor Diocletian blamed greedy sellers and capped over a thousand prices and wages.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.625
Charge more, and you could die.

00:01:19.575 --> 00:01:26.238
A writer of the time says blood was spilled over small things, and people were too scared to sell.

00:01:26.238 --> 00:01:28.142
The law was finally dropped.

00:01:29.091 --> 00:01:34.227
Wren needed luck to find a cave of pentaja. An emperor owned one.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:39.362
It was called the mint. Next: is there money with no cave at all?

